The reason is not possessiveness. Wardlatch tells people whether their machine is exposed, and its answers are
only worth anything if the person reading one can trace it to the code and the receipts that produced it. Two
different tools answering to the same name makes that impossible, and the person who loses is the one who
trusted the verdict.

The eMudhra incident data (the 12 public test hosts, the affected roots and the fix guidance) comes from the
public advisory at https://emudhra.we-have-a-problem.com/ (source: https://github.com/galadran/we-have-a-problem.com).
Wardlatch only connects to those test hosts the way any browser visiting the advisory's #check page does.
The two root certificates in lib/incidents/emudhra-2026-10-roots.pem are public CA certificates, included so the
positive control can force them in.
